WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.480
So, we’re braving the elements Brady, to
repeat the experiment that we did quite some

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.410
time ago, so you will remember that we had
a very small balloon of hydrogen

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.440
Match on a stick to hydrogen, big bang coming!

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.049
Are you ready?

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.049
Yep!

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.530
Here we go!

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:24.170
And the fact that we’ve come outside, might
actually give you an idea of what we are going

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:29.830
to do, cause we are going to make it a little
bit bigger, so let’s go and fill a balloon.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:30.900
Alright, cool.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.051
Well, hydrogen, I think is very important
because, it is the simplest atom, it’s the

00:00:37.051 --> 00:00:44.860
atom, consists of a positively charged proton
which is relatively large and a negatively

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.890
charged electron, that goes round it.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.079
Electric match?

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:54.640
I think an electric match will go well on
that.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.640
Yep.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:56.640
Oh, look at the tension on the balloon!

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.810
Hold it, I’ve really gone for it and blown
that one up.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.340
The tension!

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:09.200
What happens is that hydrogen is reacting
with oxygen and H2 plus O2 reacts to form

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:16.350
H2O and an oxygen atom and then that oxygen
atom goes on to react with another hydrogen

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:25.130
molecule to make more water and the reaction
generates a lot of heat and that heat, heats

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:31.630
up more gas to make it react faster, so you
generate more and more heat, so the reaction

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:36.369
that begins quite slowly with the first few
molecules, runs away and that is why you get

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:37.750
the explosion.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.060
So here we have a rather large balloon of
hydrogen, Neil is just preparing our electric

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.549
match which we are going to attach to the
side of this balloon.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:55.380
And some of the biggest explosions in the
20th century, the Chernobyl reactor, the space

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.969
shuttle that blew up when it was taking off,
were in fact explosions caused by hydrogen

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:02.159
and oxygen.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.450
So we’ve filled up a balloon of hydrogen,
and I think if you look at the hydrogen balloon

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:12.292
you will see that it is slightly larger than
those we have attempted before. Now we are

00:02:12.292 --> 00:02:18.260
going to try to detonate it or burn the hydrogen,
in the air by giving it a match and the match

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:24.870
we are going to use is again an electric match,
which is connected to our detonator box.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:32.140
If you stick a pin in the balloon, the balloon
will burst because the rubber will break,

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.561
but the hydrogen will come out and it won’t
react with the oxygen because the hydrogen

00:02:36.561 --> 00:02:45.450
is cool, it’s at the same temperature as
the air, but the reaction of hydrogen and

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:52.599
oxygen like many reactions involves so called
activation energy, you have to put in a certain

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:58.569
amount of energy to get it going. But once
you get it going, you get more heat coming

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:02.580
out, than you put in to start it off.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.240
So, let’s arm the circuit, 5-4-3-2-1.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:14.830
That was more like it.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:15.830
Lovely!

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:23.080
When Pete puts the match to the balloon, first
of all the match burns a hole in the balloon,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:30.650
and then the heat gets the hydrogen and oxygen
near the outside of the balloon and they start

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:37.250
reacting and the heat then spreads through
the volume of the hydrogen and oxygen, faster

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:43.769
probably than the balloon is bursting and
as it spreads through the temperature builds

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:49.069
up and up and up because you are generating
so much heat.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:57.739
The reason that you hear a noise, is because,
it causes a rise in pressure which sends a

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:04.220
pressure wave out through the air and it hits
your ears or the microphone on your camera.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:14.700
I honestly don’t know why you see any colour.
I suspect that the colour may actually be

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:22.250
caused by the burning balloon which contains
carbon and is really like the flame on a candle.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:29.820
Because normally when you have a hydrogen/oxygen
flame you don’t see any colour at all. Alternatively,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.990
it may be some impurities in the gas, but
I suspect that what you are seeing in the

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:41.460
fire ball is just the burning of the balloon.
The other thing which is really quite exciting

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:51.180
about hydrogen is that in principle if you
heat hydrogen to really high temperatures,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:05:01.199
you can get two hydrogen atoms to fuse together,
or rather, it will not work with hydrogen,

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:08.410
but it will work with a heavier form of hydrogen
which is know as deuterium where instead of

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:17.630
a proton and an electron, you have a proton
and a neutron, which is a neutral particle,

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:27.660
and an electron, and two molecules of deuterium
can fuse together to make one atom of helium

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:35.630
which has two protons, two neutrons, and two
electrons and this produces a lot of energy.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:42.449
It is the basis of the so-called hydrogen
bombs, which are like super atomic bombs,

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:47.039
but which require an enormously high temperature
to get the reaction to go.

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:51.860
You can probably hear it hitting and fizzing
into the bottle there.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:06:00.039
There is great hope that in the end much of
the world’s energy crisis can be solved

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:07.940
by doing this so called nuclear fusion of
deuterium to make helium which would produce

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.530
energy extremely cleanly.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:16.889
So here, we’ll just tie it off, if my Welsh
hands can actually tie a knot on a balloon,

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:18.940
‘cause, you know we are all a little bit
bizarre.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:26.930
Deuterium gas, in all its properties, will
be very similar to that of hydrogen, of course

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:33.020
it is denser, because it has a neutron as
well as a proton but it is still much lighter

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:39.400
than air so a deuterium balloon will still
float up to the ceiling and make Pete look

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.199
just as stupid as the hydrogen one.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:44.570
You’ve got to keep hold of it.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.500
Or you lose it

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.400
Or you lose it, you know!

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:53.819
I was thinking more of fusing it, that sounds
like a really big explosion we could do out

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:54.819
the back.

00:06:54.819 --> 00:07:00.580
No! Fusing reactors are way beyond what Pete
can do unless he is a lot cleverer than I

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:01.580
think.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:04.690
I got a balloon! I had a balloon of hydrogen!

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:10.080
That is the second time you have done that!

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.440
Now it’s going to go up there eventually
anyway!

